Coupled Safety and Recoverability Boundaries in Heterogeneous Multi-Agent Planetary Autonomy.

CVA-Space v0.4

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Chatgpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Grok XAi.

Abstract

We develop a corrected formal theory for the safety and recoverability of heterogeneous multi-agent planetary autonomous systems under structured perturbations. Building on CVA-Space v0.3, this revision addresses five load-bearing gaps: (i) the under-specification of the guaranteed domain in Theorem I; (ii) the missing policy quantifier in the irreversibility index; (iii) the unrealizability of product reference measures on projected coordinates; (iv) the unjustified  C^3  regularity of conditional-expectation functionals; and (v) the non-absorbability of the fallback policy into geometric invariant erosion. We ground each revision in established mathematics: the Hoeffding/functional ANOVA decomposition (Hoeffding 1948; Stone 1994; Hooker 2007), supermodularity and lattice theory (Topkis 1978, 1998), stochastic viability theory (Aubin 1991; Doyen & De Lara 2010), Backup Control Barrier Functions (Ames et al.; Kim et al. 2026), and measure-theoretic conditional expectation as orthogonal projection (Kallenberg 2021). The theory remains purely theoretical: every claim is falsifiable by proof, certified bound, or counterexample construction within the specified model class.

---

1. Introduction, Epistemic Status, and Method

1.1 Motivation

Heterogeneous multi-agent planetary autonomy combines light-time delays from seconds (lunar) to tens of minutes (Mars–Earth), correlated thermal and radiation failures, and planner uncertainty from large language models, symbolic planners, and stochastic search procedures. Safety cannot be reduced to scalar latency or scalar failure probability. The scientific question is whether the boundaries of safety and recoverability deform in ways that cannot be reconstructed from marginal and pairwise effects.

