8. Recoverability and the Safety–Recoverability Gap

D14 (Stochastic viability kernel). For fixed  (\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})  and constraint set  \mathcal{S} ,

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S})
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{S}:\ \exists\pi\ \text{s.t.}\
\mathbb{P}\!\left(\forall t\ge0,\ x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}\mid x_0=x,\pi,\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}\right)\ge 1-\epsilon
\right\}.

Remark 8.1. The stochastic viability kernel is an established object in viability theory. CVA-Space does not introduce recoverability or the viability kernel.

D15 (Recoverability region and boundary).

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}})=\{\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}:x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})\},\qquad
\partial\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}).

D16 (Finite-horizon recoverability with terminal set). For horizon  H  and terminal safe set  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}\subseteq\mathcal{S} ,

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}})
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{S}:\ \exists\pi\ \text{s.t.}\
\mathbb{P}\!\left(\forall t\le H,\ x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}\ \wedge\ x_H^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}\right)\ge 1-\epsilon
\right\}.

Lemma 8.1 (Monotonicity of the viability kernel in the constraint set). Let  \mathcal{S}_1\subseteq\mathcal{S}_2\subseteq\mathcal{X} . Then

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S}_1)\subseteq\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S}_2).

Proof. Fix  x\in\mathcal{S}_1\subseteq\mathcal{S}_2  and  \pi . The event  E_1=\{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_1\}  is a subset of  E_2=\{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_2\} . Hence  \mathbb{P}(E_1)\le\mathbb{P}(E_2) . If  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_1) , there exists  \pi  such that  \mathbb{P}(E_1)\ge1-\epsilon . The same  \pi  gives  \mathbb{P}(E_2)\ge1-\epsilon , so  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_2) .  \square 

Remark 8.2. Lemma 8.1 is a consequence of the definition, not an independent assumption.

P1 (Nesting of regions).

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}})\subseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}).

Proof. Let  \Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}} , so  x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}) . There exists  \pi  with  \mathbb{P}(\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S})\ge1-\epsilon . The event  \{\forall t\ge0\}\subseteq\{\forall t\le H\}=\neg V_H , hence  \mathbb{P}(V_H)\le\epsilon , so  \Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}} .  \square 

Remark 8.3. The finite-horizon kernel  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}})  with strict terminal set  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}\subsetneq\mathcal{S}  does not in general contain  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}} . The monotone relation

\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}\subseteq\mathcal{S}\ \Longrightarrow\ \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}})\subseteq\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S})

holds by the same argument as Lemma 8.1.

D17 (Gap).

\mathcal{G}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}):=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}})\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}),

