11. Theorem II: Irreversibility (Corrected)

11.1 Corrected Irreversibility Index

The earlier definition of the irreversibility index (D24) failed to specify the policy followed after the initial action  a_0 . We correct this by quantifying over admissible future policies.

D23 (Irreversibility certificate — corrected). For horizon  H  and threshold  \delta\in(0,1) ,

a\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{irr}}^{(H,\delta)}(x;\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})
\iff
\exists t\le H:\ \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}}\ \mathbb{P}\bigl(x_t\notin\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})\mid x_0=x,a_0=a,\pi\bigr)>\delta.

Here  \Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}  denotes the class of admissible future policies.

D24 (Irreversibility index — corrected).

I(a,x;\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}):=\sup_{t\le H}\ \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}}\ \mathbb{P}\bigl(x_t\notin\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}\mid x_0=x,a_0=a,\pi\bigr).

Remark 11.1. Irreversibility is a dynamical property of the transition system and the viability kernel, not a semantic label attached to actions. An action is irreversible exactly to the extent that it destroys future viability, under the worst admissible future policy.

11.2 Primitive Order Conditions

A6 (Primitive order hypotheses on irreversibility). Let  \{\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I)\}_{I\ge0}  be a family of design configurations parameterized by an irreversibility level  I . Suppose:

· (P-ORD) The admissible action sets are nested: for  I'\ge I ,
  \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}(I')\subseteq\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}(I).
· (P-KER) The transition kernel restricted to common admissible actions is  I -independent: for all  a\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}(I')  and all  x ,
  T(x'\mid x,a;\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I'))=T(x'\mid x,a;\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I)).

Remark 11.2. A6 replaces the earlier A-IRR with primitive structural conditions. The nesting of admissible action sets encodes irreversibility as permanent restriction of control authority.

P4 (Viability contraction from primitive order). Under A6, for  I'\ge I ,

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I'),\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})
\subseteq
\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I),\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}).

Proof. Fix  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I'),\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})  and let  \pi'  be a policy witnessing  x . By construction  \pi'  uses only actions in  \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}(I')\subseteq\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}(I) , hence  \pi'  is admissible under  \Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I) . By P-KER, the transition kernel under  \pi'  coincides with that under  \Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I') . Hence the event  \{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi'}\in\mathcal{S}\}  has the same probability under both configurations, and in particular is at least  1-\epsilon  under  \Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I) . Thus  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I),\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}) .  \square 

A7 (Instantaneous invariant independence). For all  I ,

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I))=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(0)).

Remark 11.3. A7 is not automatic. Many irreversible actions alter  \phi  as well as future viability. Systems that violate A7 are discussed in §13.

Theorem II (Irreversibility widens the gap — corrected). Under A6 and A7, for  I'\ge I ,

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I'))\subseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I)),
\qquad
\mathcal{G}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I'))\supseteq\mathcal{G}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}(I)).

Proof. By P4,  x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I')  implies  x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I) , so  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I')\subseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I) . By A7,  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(I')=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(I) . Hence

\mathcal{G}(I')=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I')\supseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I)=\mathcal{G}(I).

 \square 

