12. Theorem III: Coupled Interaction (Corrected)

12.1 Corrected Projected Reference Measure

The earlier formulation of A8 assumed a product measure on the full perturbation space  \Theta_{\mathrm{pert}} . This is problematic because  D,F,U  are projections, and a product measure on projections need not arise as a pushforward of any measure on the full space. We correct this by defining the reference measure directly on the projected space.

D25 (Scalar projection via conditional expectation — corrected). For pre-registered scalar projections

\sigma_D:\mathrm{supp}(\Theta_{\mathrm{comm}})\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0},\quad
\sigma_F:\mathrm{supp}(F)\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0},\quad
\sigma_U:\mathrm{supp}(U)\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0},

and a reference measure  \mu  on  \mathrm{supp}(\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}) , define

A_\sigma(d,f,u):=\mathbb{E}_\mu\bigl[A(\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}})\mid \sigma_D(\Theta_{\mathrm{comm}})=d,\ \sigma_F(F)=f,\ \sigma_U(U)=u\bigr].

Remark 12.1. The inverse-projection notation  A(\sigma_D^{-1}(D),\dots)  is ill-posed, since a projection is not invertible. The conditional expectation above acknowledges explicitly the information lost under projection and remains well defined as long as the conditioning events have positive measure. Conditional expectation is rigorously characterized as the  L^2 -orthogonal projection onto the subspace of  \sigma -measurable functions.

A8 (Reference measure for causal decomposition — corrected). For the primary interaction analysis, the projected reference measure

\mu_{DFU}:=\text{law of }(\sigma_D(\Theta_{\mathrm{comm}}),\sigma_F(F),\sigma_U(U))\text{ under }\mu

is assumed to be a product measure  \mu_{DFU}=\mu_D\otimes\mu_F\otimes\mu_U . This is an assumption about the projected measure, not about the full perturbation measure  \mu . Natural correlations between failure modes and delays are studied in a secondary analysis using a coupled projected measure  \mu_{DFU}^{\mathrm{corr}}\neq\mu_D\otimes\mu_F\otimes\mu_U .

Remark 12.2. A8 resolves the tension between the correlated failure law  \rho_F  (A4) and the product-measure assumption of the Hoeffding decomposition. The primary decomposition isolates causal interaction structure; the secondary decomposition isolates statistical dependence.

12.2 Regularity of  A_\sigma 

The interaction operator  \mathfrak{I}_{DFU}[A]=\partial_d\partial_f\partial_u A  requires  A_\sigma\in C^3 . Conditional expectations of measurable functions need not be  C^3 . We therefore either assume this regularity explicitly or work entirely with finite differences.

D26 (Interaction operator — derivative-free version). Define the third-order finite-difference interaction

\Delta_{DFU}:=A_\sigma(D,F,U)-A_\sigma(D,F,0)-A_\sigma(D,0,U)-A_\sigma(0,F,U)
+A_\sigma(D,0,0)+A_\sigma(0,F,0)+A_\sigma(0,0,U)-A_\sigma(0,0,0).

For the smooth model idealization, when  A_\sigma\in C^3 , the mixed derivative  \mathfrak{I}_{DFU}[A]=\partial_d\partial_f\partial_u A  exists and  \Delta_{DFU}\to\mathfrak{I}_{DFU}[A]\cdot\Delta D\,\Delta F\,\Delta U  as increments vanish.

A9 (Regularity hypothesis — optional). If the derivative form is used, we assume  A_\sigma\in C^3  on the pre-registered box. This is an explicit regularity hypothesis, not a consequence of the conditional expectation definition.

12.3 Corrected Interaction Separation

The earlier P6 claimed that derivative operator difference implies finite-difference difference. This is only true for sufficiently small increments or under a quantitative stability bound. We correct this.

P5 (Supermodularity criterion — corrected). If  \Delta_{DFU}>0  for all increments in the box, then no additive or pairwise model of  A_\sigma  can reproduce  A_\sigma  on that box. Conversely, if  A_\sigma  is additive, then  \Delta_{DFU}=0 .

Proof. Additivity gives  \Delta_{DFU}=0  identically by direct substitution. Strict supermodularity gives  \Delta_{DFU}>0  by the definition of supermodularity of order three. Supermodularity and increasing differences are equivalent on  \mathbb{R}^N .  \square 

P6 (Interaction separation — corrected). If the finite-difference interactions differ on the pre-registered box:

\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}\neq\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}

then the scalar interactions  \tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}\neq\tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}} . In particular, a sufficient condition for  |\tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}|>|\tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}|  is that  |\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}|>|\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}|  on the same box.

Proof. The finite-difference interaction  \Delta_{DFU}  is the numerator of  \tilde\Delta_{DFU} . If the numerators differ and the increments are common, the normalized quantities differ.  \square 

Remark 12.3. The derivative form  \mathfrak{I}_{DFU}[A]  can be retained as a smooth-model idealization, but the primary statements are finite-difference.

12.4 Hoeffding Decomposition

D27 (Hoeffding decomposition — validated). Under A8, the functional ANOVA decomposition of  A  with respect to  \mu_{DFU}  is

A(z)=A_0+\sum_i A_i(z_i)+\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}(z_i,z_j)+A_{123}(z_1,z_2,z_3),

with orthogonality conditions  \int A_S(z_S)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_i(z_i)=0  for each  i\in S . The vector interaction strength is

\mathcal{I}_{123}:=\frac{\|A_{123}\|_{L^2(\mu)}}{\|A-A_0\|_{L^2(\mu)}}\in[0,1].

This decomposition is the Hoeffding/functional ANOVA decomposition, introduced by Hoeffding (1948) for independent variables, generalized by Stone (1994) and Hooker (2007) for dependent variables. Under independence (A8), the components are orthogonal and the variance decomposes exactly.

P7 (Interaction separation between safety and recoverability). Suppose the finite-difference interactions satisfy

|\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}|>|\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}|

on a pre-registered box. Then

|\tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}|>|\tilde\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}|

on the same box.

Proof. Direct from the normalization by common increments.  \square 

Theorem III (Coupled interaction — corrected). Under A8:

· (i) If  A_\sigma  is strictly supermodular of order three on a pre-registered box, no additive or pairwise model can reproduce  A_\sigma  on that box, and  \Delta_{DFU}>0 .
· (ii) If  A_\sigma  is additive, then  \Delta_{DFU}=0  and  \mathcal{I}_{123}=0 .
· (iii) If the finite-difference interactions for safety and recoverability satisfy  |\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{rec}}|>|\Delta_{DFU}^{\mathrm{safe}}|  on a box, then the normalized interactions satisfy the same inequality.

