13. Fallback Policy and Non-Absorbable Contraction

13.1 The Structural Gap Between Erosion and Fallback

Theorem I characterizes verifier latency through geometric erosion of the safe set:  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V(\tau_V)} . This erosion constrains where the system currently is relative to constraint boundaries when a decision is verified.

However, when the runtime assurance filter rejects a candidate action, it switches control authority to the fallback policy  \pi_{\mathrm{fb}}  (D7). The viability kernel  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}  requires that there exists an admissible policy keeping the system within the safe set indefinitely (D14). The fallback policy is part of the closed-loop dynamics that defines  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}} .

P8 (Non-absorbability of the fallback contraction). The fallback-induced contraction of the viability kernel cannot in general be absorbed into the geometric erosion  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eff}} .

Proof sketch. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists an enlarged geometric margin  \rho'\ge\rho_V(\tau_V)  such that

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho'})=\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^{\pi_{\mathrm{fb}}}(\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V(\tau_V)}),

where  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^{\pi_{\mathrm{fb}}}  denotes the viability kernel under the closed-loop dynamics induced by  \pi_{\mathrm{fb}} . The left-hand side depends only on the geometry of the constraint set; the right-hand side depends on the dynamics of  \pi_{\mathrm{fb}} . For a fixed constraint geometry, varying  \pi_{\mathrm{fb}}  changes the right-hand side while leaving the left-hand side unchanged. Hence no single  \rho'  can equalize the two for all  \pi_{\mathrm{fb}} .  \square 

Remark 13.1. This result formalizes the architectural insight that the fallback policy induces a distinct, non-absorbable contraction of the recoverable set. The literature on Backup Control Barrier Functions confirms that the backup policy defines an implicit safe set that is structurally distinct from the nominal safe set.

13.2 Backup CBF Formulation

The Backup CBF framework defines the implicit safe set as the recoverable set induced by the backup policy. The backup CBF  h_{\mathrm{BCBF}}  is defined from the minimum safety margin observed along the predicted backup trajectory. This confirms that the fallback/backup policy creates a set-theoretic restriction on the viability kernel that is not purely geometric.

