18. Conclusion

We have presented a corrected formal theory of coupled safety and recoverability boundaries for heterogeneous multi-agent planetary autonomy. The framework replaces scalar latency with a heterogeneous communication configuration, scalar failure probability with a possibly correlated joint law, and scalar planner uncertainty with a decomposed triple.

Theorem I (Delayed verification) separates metric error from probabilistic error in the verifier, proves that the verifier's guaranteed domain contains an erosion of the safe set, and shows that the viability kernel is monotone nonincreasing in the verifier latency. The guaranteed domain is correctly defined to quantify over all verifier estimates within the geometric uncertainty ball.

Theorem II (Irreversibility) derives the contraction of the viability kernel from primitive order-theoretic conditions on admissible action sets and transition kernels. The irreversibility index is correctly specified with a supremum over admissible future policies.

Theorem III (Coupled interaction) defines an interaction operator via conditional expectation, provides supermodularity and additivity criteria for its vanishing or non-vanishing, and states sufficient conditions under which the interaction strength in  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}  strictly exceeds that in  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}} . The reference measure is defined on the projected space, and the derivative form is replaced by finite differences as the primary statement.

Fallback non-absorbability (P8) formalizes the architectural insight that the fallback policy induces a distinct, non-absorbable contraction of the recoverable set.

The mathematical foundations are established: Hoeffding/functional ANOVA for interaction decomposition, supermodularity and lattice theory for interaction certification, stochastic viability theory for recoverability kernels, Backup CBFs for fallback policy analysis, and measure-theoretic conditional expectation for projection.

The remaining work is to discharge or weaken the strongest remaining assumptions (especially A2, A6, A7, and the transversality condition), close the quantitative gaps between vector interaction strength and its scalar projections, and produce at least one non-trivial certified bound or counterexample inside a rigorously related abstraction.



