Appendix A: Measure-Theoretic Foundations

A.1 Probability Spaces and Conditional Expectation

A.1.1 (Probability space). Let  (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})  be a complete probability space. All random objects are  \mathcal{F} -measurable.

A.1.2 (Conditional expectation as orthogonal projection). Let  \mathcal{G}\subseteq\mathcal{F}  be a sub- \sigma -algebra and  X\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}) . The conditional expectation  \mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]  is the unique  \mathcal{G} -measurable random variable  Y  satisfying

\mathbb{E}[(X-Y)Z]=0\quad\text{for all }Z\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal{G},\mathbb{P}).

Equivalently,  \mathbb{E}[\cdot\mid\mathcal{G}]  is the orthogonal projection of  L^2(\mathcal{F})  onto the closed subspace  L^2(\mathcal{G}) .

Proof. Existence and uniqueness follow from the Hilbert projection theorem. The subspace  L^2(\mathcal{G})  is closed in  L^2(\mathcal{F})  because  \mathcal{G} -measurable functions form a complete subspace. The projection  Y  is characterized by  \langle X-Y,Z\rangle=0  for all  Z\in L^2(\mathcal{G}) , which is exactly the defining property of conditional expectation.  \square 

A.1.3 (Tower property). For  \mathcal{H}\subseteq\mathcal{G}\subseteq\mathcal{F} ,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\mid\mathcal{H}\bigr]=\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{H}].

Proof. Both sides are  \mathcal{H} -measurable. For any  Z\in L^2(\mathcal{H}) ,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\mid\mathcal{H}]Z\bigr]
=\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]Z]
=\mathbb{E}[XZ],

where the first equality uses the defining property of  \mathbb{E}[\cdot\mid\mathcal{H}]  and the second uses  Z\in L^2(\mathcal{H})\subseteq L^2(\mathcal{G}) . Since  \mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{H}]  is the unique  \mathcal{H} -measurable random variable with this property, the result follows.  \square 

A.1.4 (Conditional Jensen inequality). For a convex function  \varphi:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}  with  \varphi(X)\in L^1 ,

\varphi\bigl(\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\bigr)\le\mathbb{E}[\varphi(X)\mid\mathcal{G}].

Proof. Standard. See Kallenberg (2021, Theorem 6.14).  \square 

A.2 Product Measures and Fubini–Tonelli

A.2.1 (Product measure). Let  (E_i,\mathcal{E}_i,\mu_i) ,  i=1,2,3 , be  \sigma -finite measure spaces. The product measure  \mu=\mu_1\otimes\mu_2\otimes\mu_3  on  (E_1\times E_2\times E_3,\mathcal{E}_1\otimes\mathcal{E}_2\otimes\mathcal{E}_3)  is the unique measure satisfying

\mu(A_1\times A_2\times A_3)=\mu_1(A_1)\mu_2(A_2)\mu_3(A_3)

for all  A_i\in\mathcal{E}_i .

A.2.2 (Fubini–Tonelli). If  f:E_1\times E_2\times E_3\to\mathbb{R}  is  \mathcal{E}_1\otimes\mathcal{E}_2\otimes\mathcal{E}_3 -measurable and either  f\ge0  or  f\in L^1(\mu) , then

\int f\,\mathrm{d}\mu
=\int_{E_1}\int_{E_2}\int_{E_3}f(x_1,x_2,x_3)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_3(x_3)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_2(x_2)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_1(x_1),

and the iterated integrals are equal in any order.

Proof. Standard. See Kallenberg (2021, Theorem 3.4).  \square 

A.3 Pushforward Measures and Projections

A.3.1 (Pushforward). Let  T:(E,\mathcal{E})\to(F,\mathcal{F})  be measurable. The pushforward  T_*\mu  on  (F,\mathcal{F})  is defined by

(T_*\mu)(B)=\mu(T^{-1}(B)),\qquad B\in\mathcal{F}.

A.3.2 (Projection map). Define  \pi_i:E_1\times E_2\times E_3\to E_i  by  \pi_i(x_1,x_2,x_3)=x_i . The marginal  \mu_i  is  (\pi_i)_*\mu  when  \mu=\mu_1\otimes\mu_2\otimes\mu_3 .

A.3.3 (Conditional expectation given a projection). For  X\in L^2(E_1\times E_2\times E_3,\mu)  and the projection  \pi_1 ,

\mathbb{E}_\mu[X\mid\pi_1=x_1]=\int_{E_2\times E_3}X(x_1,x_2,x_3)\,\mathrm{d}(\mu_2\otimes\mu_3)(x_2,x_3)

when  \mu=\mu_1\otimes\mu_2\otimes\mu_3 . This is the disintegration of  \mu  along  \pi_1 .

Proof. The right-hand side is  \sigma(\pi_1) -measurable. For any  A\in\mathcal{E}_1  and  Z=\mathbf{1}_{A\times E_2\times E_3} ,

\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{E_2\times E_3}X(x_1,x_2,x_3)\,\mathrm{d}(\mu_2\otimes\mu_3)\cdot Z\right]
=\int_{A}\int_{E_2\times E_3}X\,\mathrm{d}(\mu_2\otimes\mu_3)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_1
=\mathbb{E}[XZ]

by Fubini–Tonelli. Uniqueness of conditional expectation gives the result.  \square 

