Appendix B: Full Proofs for Theorem I (Delayed Verification)

B.1 Proof of Lemma 4.1 (Delay-to-Inconsistency Bound)

Lemma 4.1. Assume the state trajectory  t\mapsto x_t  is  L_x -Lipschitz on the relevant interval, and that estimation adds error bounded by  \varepsilon_k . Then

\|x_t-\hat x_t^{(k)}\|\le L_x\tau_k(t)+\varepsilon_k+\|\nu_k\|_\infty.

Proof. Let  \hat x_t^{(k)}=h_k(x_{t-\tau_k(t)},\nu_k(t)) . We decompose

\|x_t-\hat x_t^{(k)}\|
\le \|x_t-x_{t-\tau_k(t)}\|+\|x_{t-\tau_k(t)}-h_k(x_{t-\tau_k(t)},\nu_k(t))\|.

The first term is bounded by  L_x\tau_k(t)  by the Lipschitz assumption. The second term is bounded by the estimation error  \varepsilon_k  plus the sensing noise contribution  \|\nu_k\|_\infty , by the definition of  h_k . Combining yields the bound.  \square 

B.1.1 (Tightness). The bound is tight when the trajectory achieves its maximal Lipschitz rate in the direction of the estimation error and the estimation error is aligned with the sensing noise. In general, the bound is conservative.

B.2 Proof of Proposition P2 (Sufficiency of the Eroded Safe Set)

P2. Suppose A2 holds with model error  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}} . Let  x_t\in\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V(\tau_V)} , suppose  \|\hat x_t^V-x_t\|\le\rho_V(\tau_V) ,  \hat x_t^V\in\mathcal{S} , and the verifier approves  a . Then

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\phi(x_{t+1})=1\bigr)\ge 1-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}.

Furthermore,  \mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V(\tau_V)}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}} .

Proof. Let  x_t\in\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V} . By definition of the erosion,

\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V}=\{x\in\mathcal{S}:B_{\rho_V}(x)\subseteq\mathcal{S}\}.

Hence every  y\in B_{\rho_V}(x_t)  satisfies  y\in\mathcal{S} . Since  \|\hat x_t^V-x_t\|\le\rho_V , we have  \hat x_t^V\in B_{\rho_V}(x_t)\subseteq\mathcal{S} . The verifier estimate satisfies the antecedent of A2:

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\phi(x_{t+1})=1\mid \hat x_t^V\in\mathcal{S},a\text{ approved}\bigr)\ge 1-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}.

Since  \hat x_t^V  and  a  are given, the conditional probability is well-defined. For the inclusion: let  x\in\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V}  and  \hat x\in B_{\rho_V}(x)  with  \hat x\in\mathcal{S} . By A2,  \mathbb{P}(\phi(x_{t+1})=1\mid \hat x,a\text{ approved})\ge 1-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}} . Since this holds for all such  \hat x , the definition of  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}  is satisfied.  \square 

B.2.1 (Remark on measurability). The set  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}  is measurable provided  \mathcal{S}  is Borel and the map  \hat x\mapsto\mathbb{P}(\phi(x_{t+1})=1\mid \hat x,a\text{ approved})  is Borel-measurable. This holds under standard regularity conditions on the transition kernel  T .

B.3 Proof of Corollary 9.1 (Equality Under Attainability)

Corollary 9.1. Suppose additionally that:

· (i) The verifier's estimate error can realize any  v\in B_{\rho_V}  in some realization.
· (ii) The verifier's acceptance region is exactly  \mathcal{S}  up to the invariant boundary.
· (iii) No additional filter margin is introduced.

Then  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}=\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V(\tau_V)} .

Proof. The inclusion  \mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}  is P2. For the reverse inclusion, let  x\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}} . Suppose for contradiction that  x\notin\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V} . Then there exists  y\in B_{\rho_V}(x)  with  y\notin\mathcal{S} . By (i), there is a realization with  \hat x_t^V=y . By (ii), the verifier rejects  y\notin\mathcal{S} , so no approval occurs. But  x\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}  requires that for all  \hat x\in B_{\rho_V}(x)\cap\mathcal{S} , approval implies the invariant. If  y\notin\mathcal{S} , the condition is vacuous for  y , but (i) and (ii) together imply that the verifier cannot certify  x  because the estimate could be  y . More precisely, if  x\notin\mathcal{S}\ominus B_{\rho_V} , there exists  \hat x\in B_{\rho_V}(x)  with  \hat x\notin\mathcal{S} . By (i), such  \hat x  is realized in some execution. By (ii), the verifier does not approve. Hence the approval condition in  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}}  is not satisfied for this realization, and  x\notin\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{guaranteed}} . Contrapositive gives the reverse inclusion.  \square 

