Proof. Fix  \omega  and  I'\ge I . Let  r>0  be such that  \theta_0+r\omega\in\mathcal{G}(I) . Then  \theta_0+r\omega\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}  and  \theta_0+r\omega\notin\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I) . By Theorem II,  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I')\subseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I) , so  \theta_0+r\omega\notin\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(I') . Also  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}  is fixed, so  \theta_0+r\omega\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}} . Hence  \theta_0+r\omega\in\mathcal{G}(I') . Therefore  r_{\mathcal{G}}(\omega;I')\ge r_{\mathcal{G}}(\omega;I) .  \square 

C.4 Proof of Lemma 8.1 (Monotonicity of the Viability Kernel)

Lemma 8.1. Let  \mathcal{S}_1\subseteq\mathcal{S}_2\subseteq\mathcal{X} . Then

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S}_1)
\subseteq
\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}};\mathcal{S}_2).

Proof. Fix  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_1) . There exists  \pi  such that

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\forall t\ge0,\ x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}_1\mid x_0=x,\pi\bigr)\ge 1-\epsilon.

The event  E_1=\{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}_1\}  is a subset of  E_2=\{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}_2\}  because  \mathcal{S}_1\subseteq\mathcal{S}_2 . Hence

\mathbb{P}(E_2)\ge\mathbb{P}(E_1)\ge 1-\epsilon.

Therefore  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_2) .  \square 

C.5 Proof of Proposition P1 (Nesting of Regions)

P1. Under the definitions above,  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}})\subseteq\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}}) .

Proof. Let  \Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{rec}} . Then  x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\Theta_{\mathrm{des}},\Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}) . There exists  \pi  with

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}\bigr)\ge 1-\epsilon.

The event  \{\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}\}  is a subset of  \{\forall t\le H:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S}\}=\neg V_H . Hence

\mathbb{P}(V_H)\le 1-\mathbb{P}(\forall t\ge0:\,x_t^{\pi}\in\mathcal{S})\le\epsilon.

Therefore  \Theta_{\mathrm{pert}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{safe}} .  \square 

