Appendix D: Full Details of the Hoeffding Decomposition

D.1 Construction of the Orthogonal Components

D.1.1 (Setup). Let  (E_i,\mathcal{E}_i,\mu_i) ,  i=1,2,3 , be probability spaces. Let  E=E_1\times E_2\times E_3 ,  \mu=\mu_1\otimes\mu_2\otimes\mu_3 , and  A\in L^2(E,\mu) .

D.1.2 (Projection operators). For  S\subseteq\{1,2,3\} , define the projection operator  P_S:L^2(E,\mu)\to L^2(E,\mu)  by

(P_S A)(z)=\mathbb{E}_\mu[A\mid z_i,i\in S],

where the conditional expectation is with respect to the  \sigma -algebra generated by  \{z_i:i\in S\} .

D.1.3 (Properties of projection operators).

· (i)  P_S  is a linear orthogonal projection:  P_S^2=P_S  and  P_S^*=P_S .
· (ii)  P_SP_T=P_{S\cap T} .
· (iii)  P_{\emptyset}A=\mathbb{E}_\mu[A]=A_0 .
· (iv)  P_{\{1,2,3\}}A=A .

Proof. (i)  P_S  is the orthogonal projection onto  L^2(\sigma(z_i,i\in S)) . (ii) By the tower property,  P_SP_T=P_{S\cap T} . (iii) and (iv) are immediate.  \square 

D.1.4 (Hoeffding components). Define

A_0=P_{\emptyset}A,\qquad
A_i=(P_{\{i\}}-P_{\emptyset})A,\qquad
A_{ij}=(P_{\{i,j\}}-P_{\{i\}}-P_{\{j\}}+P_{\emptyset})A,

A_{123}=(P_{\{1,2,3\}}-P_{\{1,2\}}-P_{\{1,3\}}-P_{\{2,3\}}+P_{\{1\}}+P_{\{2\}}+P_{\{3\}}-P_{\emptyset})A.

D.1.5 (Orthogonality). For distinct subsets  S,T\subseteq\{1,2,3\}  with  S\not\subseteq T  and  T\not\subseteq S ,

\langle A_S,A_T\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}=0.

Proof. It suffices to show  \mathbb{E}[A_S A_T]=0 . By construction,  A_S  is  \sigma(z_i,i\in S) -measurable and  A_T  is  \sigma(z_i,i\in T) -measurable. Under the product measure,  \sigma(z_i,i\in S)  and  \sigma(z_i,i\in T)  are independent if  S\cap T=\emptyset . If  S\cap T\neq\emptyset , the orthogonality follows from the vanishing of the conditional expectations:  \mathbb{E}[A_S\mid z_i,i\in S\cap T]=0  and similarly for  A_T . A direct computation using the tower property gives  \mathbb{E}[A_S A_T]=0 .  \square 

D.2 Variance Decomposition

D.2.1 (Total variance). Define

\sigma_T^2=\mathrm{Var}_\mu(A)=\|A-A_0\|_{L^2(\mu)}^2.

D.2.2 (Component variances).

\sigma_S^2=\|A_S\|_{L^2(\mu)}^2,\qquad S\subseteq\{1,2,3\},\ S\neq\emptyset.

D.2.3 (Variance decomposition theorem).

\sigma_T^2=\sum_{i=1}^3\sigma_i^2+\sum_{i<j}\sigma_{ij}^2+\sigma_{123}^2.

Proof. By D.1.4,

A-A_0=\sum_{i}A_i+\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}+A_{123}.

The terms are mutually orthogonal by D.1.5. Hence

\|A-A_0\|^2=\sum_i\|A_i\|^2+\sum_{i<j}\|A_{ij}\|^2+\|A_{123}\|^2.

 \square 

D.2.4 (Interaction strength).

\mathcal{I}_{123}:=\frac{\|A_{123}\|_{L^2(\mu)}}{\|A-A_0\|_{L^2(\mu)}}=\frac{\sigma_{123}}{\sigma_T}\in[0,1].

D.2.5 (Interpretation).  \mathcal{I}_{123}  measures the fraction of total variance attributable exclusively to the triple interaction.  \mathcal{I}_{123}=0  if and only if  A  is additive or pairwise-only.

D.3 Explicit Formulas for the Components

D.3.1 (Zeroth order).

A_0=\int_{E_1}\int_{E_2}\int_{E_3}A(z_1,z_2,z_3)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_3(z_3)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_2(z_2)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_1(z_1).

D.3.2 (First order).

A_i(z_i)=\int_{E_j}\int_{E_k}A(z_i,z_j,z_k)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_k(z_k)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_j(z_j)-A_0,

where  \{i,j,k\}=\{1,2,3\} .

D.3.3 (Second order).

A_{ij}(z_i,z_j)=\int_{E_k}A(z_i,z_j,z_k)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_k(z_k)-A_0-A_i(z_i)-A_j(z_j).

D.3.4 (Third order).

A_{123}(z_1,z_2,z_3)=A(z_1,z_2,z_3)-A_0-\sum_i A_i(z_i)-\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}(z_i,z_j).

D.4 Relation to Finite Differences

D.4.1 (Finite-difference interaction). For increments  \Delta z_1,\Delta z_2,\Delta z_3>0 ,

\Delta_{123}:=A(z_1+\Delta z_1,z_2+\Delta z_2,z_3+\Delta z_3)
-A(z_1+\Delta z_1,z_2+\Delta z_2,z_3)
-A(z_1+\Delta z_1,z_2,z_3+\Delta z_3)
-A(z_1,z_2+\Delta z_2,z_3+\Delta z_3)
+A(z_1+\Delta z_1,z_2,z_3)
+A(z_1,z_2+\Delta z_2,z_3)
+A(z_1,z_2,z_3+\Delta z_3)
-A(z_1,z_2,z_3).

D.4.2 (Relation to ANOVA). If  A  is additive or pairwise-only, then  \Delta_{123}=0  identically. Conversely, if  \Delta_{123}\neq0  for some increments, then  A_{123}\neq0 .

Proof. For additive  A(z)=f_1(z_1)+f_2(z_2)+f_3(z_3) , direct substitution gives  \Delta_{123}=0 . For pairwise  A(z)=\sum_{i<j}f_{ij}(z_i,z_j) , each pairwise term is annihilated by the third-order difference, so  \Delta_{123}=0 . Contrapositive: if  \Delta_{123}\neq0 , then  A  is not additive or pairwise, hence  A_{123}\neq0 .  \square 

D.5 Change of Reference Measure

D.5.1 (Problem). Given  \mathcal{I}_{123}  under  \mu , how does it transform under a change of measure  \mu\to\mu' ?

D.5.2 (Radon–Nikodym derivative). If  \mu'\ll\mu  with  \mathrm{d}\mu'/\mathrm{d}\mu=w\in L^2(\mu) , then the ANOVA components under  \mu'  are not simply related to those under  \mu .

D.5.3 (Open problem). A general transformation formula for  \mathcal{I}_{123}  under change of measure is not available. This is Open Problem 4 in the main text.

