Appendix E: Supermodularity and Lattice Theory

E.1 Lattices and Order

E.1.1 (Lattice). A partially ordered set  (L,\le)  is a lattice if every pair  x,y\in L  has a least upper bound  x\vee y  and a greatest lower bound  x\wedge y .

E.1.2 (Complete lattice). A lattice is complete if every subset has a supremum and infimum.

E.1.3 (Product lattice).  \mathbb{R}^n  with the componentwise order is a complete lattice.

E.2 Supermodularity

E.2.1 (Supermodular function). A function  f:L\to\mathbb{R}  on a lattice  L  is supermodular if for all  x,y\in L ,

f(x\vee y)+f(x\wedge y)\ge f(x)+f(y).

E.2.2 (Submodular function).  f  is submodular if  -f  is supermodular.

E.2.3 (Increasing differences). For  f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}  and  i\neq j ,

f(x_i',x_j')-f(x_i,x_j')-f(x_i',x_j)+f(x_i,x_j)\ge 0

for all  x_i'\ge x_i ,  x_j'\ge x_j , if and only if  f  has increasing differences in  (x_i,x_j) .

E.2.4 (Equivalence theorem). For  f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}  with  f\in C^2 ,  f  is supermodular if and only if

\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_i\partial x_j}\ge 0

for all  i\neq j .

Proof. Standard. See Topkis (1998, Theorem 2.3.1).  \square 

E.3 Third-Order Supermodularity

E.3.1 (Order-three supermodularity). A function  f:\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}  is supermodular of order three if

\Delta_{123}\ge 0

for all increments, where  \Delta_{123}  is the third-order finite difference.

E.3.2 (Mixed derivative characterization). If  f\in C^3 , then  f  is supermodular of order three if and only if

\frac{\partial^3 f}{\partial x_1\partial x_2\partial x_3}\ge 0.

Proof. By repeated application of the mean value theorem. See Topkis (1998, Chapter 2).  \square 

E.3.3 (Strict supermodularity).  f  is strictly supermodular of order three if  \Delta_{123}>0  for all increments in a pre-registered box.

E.4 Proof of P5 (Supermodularity Criterion)

P5. If  A_\sigma  is strictly supermodular of order three on a pre-registered box, then no additive or pairwise model can reproduce  A_\sigma  on that box, and  \Delta_{DFU}>0 . Conversely, if  A_\sigma  is additive, then  \Delta_{DFU}=0  and  \mathcal{I}_{123}=0 .

Proof. Additivity gives  \Delta_{DFU}=0  by direct substitution. Pairwise-only gives  \Delta_{DFU}=0  because each pairwise term is annihilated by the third-order difference. Strict supermodularity gives  \Delta_{DFU}>0  by definition. If  A_\sigma  were additive or pairwise-only, then  \Delta_{DFU}=0 , contradicting  \Delta_{DFU}>0 . Hence no such model reproduces  A_\sigma .  \square 

E.5 Topkis Monotonicity Theorem

E.5.1 (Topkis 1978). Let  L  be a complete lattice,  T  a partially ordered set,  f:L\times T\to\mathbb{R}  supermodular in  x  for each  t , and  f  has increasing differences in  (x,t) . Then the maximal and minimal maximizers of  f(\cdot,t)  over  L  are nondecreasing in  t .

E.5.2 (Application). The Topkis theorem provides the foundation for monotone comparative statics in the presence of supermodularity. In CVA-Space, it supports the ordering of interaction effects across design configurations.

