Appendix F: Stochastic Viability Theory

F.1 Viability Kernel

F.1.1 (Deterministic viability kernel). For a controlled dynamical system  \dot x=f(x,u) ,  u\in U , and constraint set  \mathcal{S} , the viability kernel is

\mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S})=\{x_0\in\mathcal{S}:\exists u(\cdot)\text{ s.t. }x(t)\in\mathcal{S}\ \forall t\ge0\}.

F.1.2 (Stochastic viability kernel). For a stochastic kernel  T  and constraint set  \mathcal{S} ,

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S})=\{x\in\mathcal{S}:\exists\pi\text{ s.t. }\mathbb{P}(\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}\mid x_0=x)\ge 1-\epsilon\}.

F.2 Dynamic Programming Characterization

F.2.1 (Value function). Define

V(x)=\sup_\pi\mathbb{P}\bigl(\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}\mid x_0=x\bigr).

F.2.2 (Bellman equation).  V  satisfies

V(x)=\sup_{a\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}}\int_{\mathcal{S}}V(x')T(\mathrm{d}x'\mid x,a)

for  x\in\mathcal{S} , with  V(x)=0  for  x\notin\mathcal{S} .

F.2.3 (Kernel as level set).

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S})=\{x\in\mathcal{S}:V(x)\ge 1-\epsilon\}.

Proof. By definition of  V  and  \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}} .  \square 

F.3 Monotonicity in the Constraint Set

F.3.1 (Proof of Lemma 8.1 — detailed). Let  \mathcal{S}_1\subseteq\mathcal{S}_2 . For  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_1) , there exists  \pi  with

\mathbb{P}(\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_1)\ge 1-\epsilon.

The event  \{\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_1\}\subseteq\{\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_2\} . Hence

\mathbb{P}(\forall t\ge0,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_2)\ge 1-\epsilon.

So  x\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}(\mathcal{S}_2) .  \square 

F.4 Finite-Horizon Kernel

F.4.1 (Definition).

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}})=\{x\in\mathcal{S}:\exists\pi\text{ s.t. }\mathbb{P}(\forall t\le H,x_t^\pi\in\mathcal{S}\wedge x_H^\pi\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}})\ge 1-\epsilon\}.

F.4.2 (Monotonicity in terminal set). If  \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}'\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}} , then

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}')\subseteq\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{rec}}^H(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{term}}).

Proof. Same argument as Lemma 8.1 applied to the finite-horizon event.  \square 

